menu "CSI node"

    choice CSI_ROLE
        prompt "Node role"
        default CSI_ROLE_STATION
        help
            STATION is the single-board topology and the one to start with: the node joins your
            existing WiFi, provokes a steady downlink stream from the access point, and reports
            CSI for it. One board, no second radio to place or power.

            RECEIVER and TRANSMITTER are the two halves of the ESP32 pair. The pair gives a link
            you fully own — you choose both endpoints and the rate is not shared with the rest of
            the house — at the cost of a second board. Only reach for it if the station role's
            reply yield turns out to be too poor.

        config CSI_ROLE_STATION
            bool "Station (single node: joins your WiFi, probes it, reports CSI)"

        config CSI_ROLE_RECEIVER
            bool "Receiver (promiscuous; measures a second ESP32's transmissions)"

        config CSI_ROLE_TRANSMITTER
            bool "Transmitter (the second board of the pair)"
    endchoice

    config CSI_NODE_ID
        int "Node ID"
        range 1 254
        default 1
        help
            Goes in every frame. 0 and 255 are reserved. Give each board a different one, or
            the server will interleave two rooms into a single history.

    menu "WiFi"
        comment "These are starting values. Once anything has been saved from the setup"
        comment "page, the stored settings win and these are only used again after an erase."

        config CSI_WIFI_SSID
            string "SSID"
            default "csi-net"

        config CSI_WIFI_PASSWORD
            string "Password"
            default ""

        config CSI_WIFI_MAX_RETRY
            int "Reconnect attempts before giving up"
            default 20

        config CSI_LOCK_BSSID
            string "Lock to this BSSID (aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff)"
            default ""
            help
                The setting that makes a mesh network usable for sensing. Empty means the node
                associates with whichever access point advertising the SSID it likes best, and a
                mesh system is free to steer it to a different one at any time. A roam changes
                the transmitting antenna, and therefore the geometry, and therefore every
                baseline the server has built — silently, with no error anywhere.

                Set it and the node associates with exactly that access point and never roams.
                A steering attempt then becomes a brief disconnect and a reconnect to the same
                AP: visible, countable, and correctly treated as a discontinuity by the server.

                The boot scan below prints the BSSIDs to choose from. Which one you pick is a
                real decision — the line between that AP and this node is the line the system
                senses along.

        config CSI_SCAN_ON_BOOT
            bool "Scan and log every AP on the SSID at boot"
            default y
            help
                Consumer mesh apps do not show you BSSIDs, so this log is usually the only way
                to find out which access points exist and how strong each one is from where the
                node sits. Costs a few seconds at boot.
    endmenu

    # Deliberately not `depends on` the role. csi_net.c is compiled into every image and reads
    # CSI_SENDER_PRIORITY unconditionally, so hiding these behind a role choice makes the
    # transmitter build fail on an undefined symbol rather than merely hiding a setting.
    menu "Uplink"
        config CSI_SERVER_HOST
            string "Server IPv4 address"
            default "192.168.1.10"
            help
                Where to send CSI frames. An address, not a hostname: DNS resolution is one
                more thing to fail on a device with no operator.

        config CSI_SERVER_PORT
            int "Server UDP port"
            default 5566

        config CSI_SENDER_PRIORITY
            int "Sender task priority"
            default 6
            help
                Above the default application priority so a frame does not sit in the ring
                behind unrelated work, and below the WiFi driver's so it cannot starve it.
    endmenu

    menu "Probe"
        depends on CSI_ROLE_STATION

        choice CSI_PROBE_METHOD
            prompt "Probe method"
            default CSI_PROBE_ICMP
            help
                A station that only listens gets CSI at whatever rate the access point happens
                to talk to it, which on an idle network is a few frames a second. The node has
                to provoke its own downlink stream; every reply it draws is one CSI sample.

            config CSI_PROBE_ICMP
                bool "ICMP echo to the gateway"
                help
                    One hop, no configuration, and the lowest jitter available — the reply's
                    last leg is your access point transmitting to you, which is exactly the link
                    being measured. Some routers rate-limit ICMP; that shows up as a low reply
                    yield in the statistics log rather than as a mystery.

            config CSI_PROBE_UDP_ECHO
                bool "UDP echo through the CSI server"
                help
                    The node sends a small datagram to the server's echo port and the server
                    bounces it back. Immune to router ICMP policy and the yield is exact because
                    we own both ends, at the cost of one extra LAN round trip of jitter. Needs
                    the server reachable from the node.
        endchoice

        config CSI_PROBE_TARGET
            string "Probe target IPv4 address"
            default ""
            depends on CSI_PROBE_ICMP
            help
                Empty means the DHCP gateway, which is what you want: it is one hop away and
                requires no configuration. Set it only to probe something else on the LAN.

                Wherever the target physically sits, the last hop of the reply is always the
                access point this node is associated with — so this measures the AP-to-node link
                by construction.

        config CSI_ECHO_PORT
            int "Server echo port"
            default 5568
            depends on CSI_PROBE_UDP_ECHO

        config CSI_PROBE_RATE_HZ
            int "Probe rate (Hz)"
            range 1 500
            default 100
            help
                Target ~80-100 Hz: far above Nyquist for the ~2 Hz cardiac band, with headroom
                for decimation. At 100 Hz this is roughly 6 KB/s each way, continuously, which
                is nothing for a router but is worth knowing about. Needs CONFIG_FREERTOS_HZ
                high enough that the period is a whole number of ticks — sdkconfig.defaults
                sets 1000.

        config CSI_PROBE_PRIORITY
            int "Prober task priority"
            default 5
            help
                Below the sender's. A late probe costs one sample; a late CSI frame costs a
                queue that never drains.
    endmenu

    menu "Receiver"
        depends on CSI_ROLE_RECEIVER

        config CSI_PEER_MAC
            string "Transmitter MAC to filter on"
            default ""
            help
                Only report CSI for frames from this source. Leave empty to capture from every
                station on the channel, which is useful for a first smoke test and misleading
                for anything else — the rate becomes whatever the neighbours are doing.
    endmenu

    menu "Transmitter"
        depends on CSI_ROLE_TRANSMITTER

        config CSI_TX_TARGET
            string "Destination IPv4 address"
            default "255.255.255.255"
            help
                Broadcast by default: nothing has to be listening, and the frame still goes out
                over the air, which is all the receiver needs.

        config CSI_TX_PORT
            int "Destination UDP port"
            default 5567

        config CSI_TX_RATE_HZ
            int "Transmit rate (Hz)"
            range 1 500
            default 100
            help
                Target ~80-100 Hz. That is far above Nyquist for the ~2 Hz cardiac band and
                leaves headroom for decimation. Needs CONFIG_FREERTOS_HZ high enough that the
                period is a whole number of ticks — sdkconfig.defaults sets 1000.
    endmenu

    menu "Setup portal"
        comment "A SoftAP and a web page for setting the SSID, the BSSID lock and the server"
        comment "address without a rebuild. Runs instead of capture, never alongside it."

        config CSI_PORTAL_IN_STATION
            bool "Also serve the setup page while capturing"
            default y
            help
                Keeps the same page reachable at the node's own address on your network, so
                changing the access point does not need a button press or a cable.

                Idle it is one task blocked on a listening socket and about 12 KB of heap, and it
                is in neither the CSI callback nor the packer. It is pinned to core 1 below the
                sender and the prober, so a request that arrives mid-capture waits for them
                rather than the other way round. Turn it off if you want the capture path to have
                nothing on it at all, and use the button instead.

                Note that it has no authentication: anyone on your network can read the node's
                settings, minus the WiFi password, and change them.

        config CSI_PORTAL_AP_PREFIX
            string "Setup network name prefix"
            default "csi-setup"
            help
                The last three octets of the board's MAC are appended, so two nodes being set up
                in the same room are distinguishable without a serial console.

        config CSI_PORTAL_AP_PASSWORD
            string "Setup network password"
            default ""
            help
                Under 8 characters means an open network, which is the default. The portal is
                only reachable while the node is unconfigured or the boot button was held, and
                an open network makes it obvious that this is a setup mode rather than something
                left running. Set a password if the node is somewhere you would rather nobody
                walked past and reconfigured.

        config CSI_PORTAL_BUTTON_GPIO
            int "Setup button GPIO"
            default 0
            help
                Held low for half a second after boot, this forces the portal even when settings
                are stored. It is the way back for a node configured onto a network that no
                longer exists, where nothing over the air can reach it.

                GPIO0 is the boot button on the usual DevKit boards. Note that it must be pressed
                *after* the board starts, not held through the reset: the ROM bootloader reads
                the same pin first and would stop in serial download mode instead.

        config CSI_PORTAL_BUTTON_WINDOW_MS
            int "How long to watch the setup button at boot (ms)"
            default 3000
            help
                The window has to be long enough to press into by hand after the board comes up,
                because holding the button through reset lands in the ROM bootloader rather than
                here. Every boot waits this long before joining, so it trades a few seconds of
                startup for a recovery path that does not need a cable.
    endmenu

    config CSI_REPORT_PERIOD_S
        int "Statistics log period (seconds)"
        default 10

endmenu
